# Lets GitHub show a "Cite this repository" button and gives the project a
# stable, machine-readable citation. It is also what Zenodo reads when a release
# is archived to mint a DOI.
#
# The name matches the other CITATION.cff files in these projects. The site uses
# "Dan" as a display byline, which is a separate thing from a citation name.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "RL-Chess-Engine — an AlphaZero-style chess engine built from scratch, and what it failed to learn"
message: "If you use this software or its findings, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Daniel
    family-names: Lichtenberger
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Danny-397/RL-Chess-Engine"
url: "https://rlchess.xyz"
abstract: >-
  A from-scratch AlphaZero-style chess engine: a residual policy-value network,
  Monte Carlo tree search with PUCT, the full 4672-move encoding, and a
  self-play training loop, with strength measured as Elo against fixed
  opponents and colours alternated so first-move advantage cannot be mistaken
  for learning. The honest result is the point of the write-up: at the compute
  available the agent remains weak, and the experiments separate the two
  candidate causes — insufficient search and an impoverished self-play signal —
  finding the signal the cheaper lever. A "draw cycle" in which self-play
  collapses into non-decisive games is reported rather than tuned away. The
  live demo deliberately plays a torch-free alpha-beta searcher and says so, so
  the deployed site does not imply the network is stronger than it is.
keywords:
  - reinforcement learning
  - monte carlo tree search
  - self-play
  - alphazero
  - chess engine
  - negative results
license: MIT
date-released: "2026-10-20"
